        }}

        .kpi-card.danger {{
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--danger) 0%, var(--tier5) 100%);
        }}

        .kpi-card.warning {{
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--warning) 0%, #c05621 100%);
        }}

        .kpi-card.success {{
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--success) 0%, #276749 100%);
        }}

        table {{
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin: 20px 0;
            font-size: 0.95em;
        }}

        th {{
            background: var(--primary);
            color: white;
            padding: 14px 12px;
            text-align: left;
            font-weight: 600;
        }}

        td {{
            padding: 12px;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
        }}

        tr:hover {{
            background: var(--light);
        }}

        .tier-1 {{ background: rgba(56, 161, 105, 0.1); }}
        .tier-2 {{ background: rgba(49, 130, 206, 0.1); }}
        .tier-3 {{ background: rgba(221, 107, 32, 0.1); }}
        .tier-4 {{ background: rgba(229, 62, 62, 0.1); }}
        .tier-5 {{ background: rgba(155, 44, 44, 0.1); }}

        .badge {{
            display: inline-block;
            padding: 4px 12px;
            border-radius: 20px;
            font-size: 0.8em;
            font-weight: 600;
        }}

        .badge-success {{ background: var(--success); color: white; }}
        .badge-info {{ background: var(--info); color: white; }}
        .badge-warning {{ background: var(--warning); color: white; }}
        .badge-danger {{ background: var(--danger); color: white; }}

        .chart-container {{
            margin: 30px 0;
            padding: 25px;
            background: #f8fafc;
            border-radius: 10px;
            border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
        }}

        .chart-container img {{
            max-width: 100%;
            height: auto;
            display: block;
            margin: 0 auto;
        }}

        .chart-grid {{
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(400px, 1fr));
            gap: 25px;
        }}

        .insight-box {{
            background: linear-gradient(135deg, #f7fafc 0%, #edf2f7 100%);
            border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 20px 0;
            border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
        }}

        .warning-box {{
            background: #fff5f5;
            border-left: 4px solid var(--danger);
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 20px 0;
            border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
        }}

        .two-column {{
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
            gap: 30px;
        }}

        .region-card {{
            background: white;
            border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
            border-radius: 10px;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 10px 0;
        }}

        .region-card h4 {{
            color: var(--primary);
            margin-bottom: 15px;
            font-size: 1.1em;
        }}

        .region-stat {{
            display: flex;
            justify-content: space-between;
            padding: 8px 0;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #f0f0f0;
        }}

        .region-stat:last-child {{
            border-bottom: none;
        }}

        .region-stat .value {{
            font-weight: 600;
            color: var(--secondary);
        }}

        .tier-summary-grid {{
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
            gap: 20px;
            margin: 25px 0;
        }}

        .tier-card {{
            border-radius: 10px;
            padding: 20px;
            color: white;
        }}

        .tier-card.t1 {{ background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--tier1), #276749); }}
        .tier-card.t2 {{ background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--tier2), #2c5282); }}
        .tier-card.t3 {{ background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--tier3), #c05621); }}
        .tier-card.t4 {{ background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--tier4), #c53030); }}
        .tier-card.t5 {{ background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--tier5), #742a2a); }}

        .tier-card h4 {{
            font-size: 1.1em;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }}

        .tier-card .stat {{
            display: flex;
            justify-content: space-between;
            padding: 6px 0;
            font-size: 0.9em;
        }}

        .tier-card .stat .label {{
            opacity: 0.9;
        }}

        .tier-card .stat .value {{
            font-weight: 600;
        }}

        .finding {{
            background: white;
            border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
            border-radius: 8px;
            padding: 20px;
            margin: 15px 0;
        }}

        .finding h4 {{
            color: var(--danger);
            margin-bottom: 10px;
        }}

        .footer {{
            background: var(--primary);
            color: white;
            padding: 30px 40px;
            text-align: center;
            font-size: 0.9em;
        }}

        .footer .disclaimer {{
            opacity: 0.8;
            margin-top: 10px;
            font-size: 0.85em;
        }}

        @media (max-width: 768px) {{
            .two-column {{
                grid-template-columns: 1fr;
            }}
            .header h1 {{
                font-size: 1.8em;
            }}
            .section {{
                padding: 25px 20px;
            }}
        }}
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="report-container">
        <div class="header">
            <h1>Global Cost of Living Crisis Index 2026</h1>
            <p class="subtitle">An analytical report examining cost-of-living pressures, affordability gaps, and economic stress indicators across 80 major cities worldwide in 2026.</p>
            <p class="meta">Dataset: 80 Cities | Baseline: New York, USA (Index = 100) | Audience: Policy Analysts & Economic Researchers</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Executive Overview -->
        <div class="section" id="executive-overview">
            <h2>Executive Overview</h2>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <div class="value">{median_coli:.1f}</div>
                    <div class="label">Median Cost of Living Index</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card warning">
                    <div class="value">{avg_rent_salary:.1f}%</div>
                    <div class="label">Avg Rent-to-Salary Ratio</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card success">
                    <div class="value">{purchasing_power_gap:.1f}</div>
                    <div class="label">Purchasing Power Gap</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card danger">
                    <div class="value">{high_crisis_count}</div>
                    <div class="label">High-Crisis City Count</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <div class="value">{avg_inflation:.1f}%</div>
                    <div class="label">Avg Annual Inflation Rate</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Key Headline:</strong> The global median cost of living index stands at <strong>{median_coli:.1f}</strong> (NY=100), indicating that half of all cities surveyed are more affordable than New York. However, the average rent-to-salary ratio of <strong>{avg_rent_salary:.1f}%</strong> globally signals a severe housing affordability crisis — workers in the average city must dedicate over 60% of their income to city-center rent.
            </div>

            <h3>Most & Least Expensive Cities</h3>
            <div class="two-column">
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: var(--danger);">Top 10 Most Expensive Cities</h4>
                    <table>
                        <tr><th>Rank</th><th>City</th><th>Country</th><th>CoL Index</th><th>Rent Burden</th></tr>
                        {''.join(f"<tr><td>{row['rank']}</td><td>{row['city']}</td><td>{row['country']}</td><td>{row['cost_of_living_index']:.1f}</td><td>{row['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct']:.1f}%</td></tr>" for _, row in most_expensive.iterrows())}
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 style="color: var(--success);">Top 10 Least Expensive Cities</h4>
                    <table>
                        <tr><th>Rank</th><th>City</th><th>Country</th><th>CoL Index</th><th>Rent Burden</th></tr>
                        {''.join(f"<tr><td>{row['rank']}</td><td>{row['city']}</td><td>{row['country']}</td><td>{row['cost_of_living_index']:.1f}</td><td>{row['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct']:.1f}%</td></tr>" for _, row in least_expensive.iterrows())}
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="warning-box" style="margin-top: 25px;">
                <strong>Critical Finding:</strong> The purchasing power gap of <strong>{purchasing_power_gap:.1f}</strong> points between the highest (Basel: 183.7) and lowest (Cairo: 42.0) purchasing power cities reveals extreme global inequality. A worker in Basel has over 4x the effective purchasing power of one in Cairo, even after accounting for local price levels.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Regional Comparison -->
        <div class="section" id="regional-comparison">
            <h2>Regional Cost-of-Living Comparison</h2>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['region_box']}" alt="Cost of Living by Region">
            </div>

            <h3>Regional Summary Statistics</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Region</th>
                    <th>Cities</th>
                    <th>Avg CoL Index</th>
                    <th>Median CoL Index</th>
                    <th>Avg Rent Index</th>
                    <th>Avg PPP Index</th>
                    <th>Avg Rent Burden</th>
                    <th>Avg Inflation</th>
                </tr>
                {''.join(f"<tr><td>{region}</td><td>{row['city_count']}</td><td>{row['cost_of_living_index_mean']:.1f}</td><td>{row['cost_of_living_index_median']:.1f}</td><td>{row['rent_index_mean']:.1f}</td><td>{row['local_purchasing_power_index_mean']:.1f}</td><td>{row['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct_mean']:.1f}%</td><td>{row['annual_inflation_rate_2025_pct_mean']:.1f}%</td></tr>" for region, row in region_summary.iterrows())}
            </table>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Regional Outlier Analysis:</strong>
                <ul style="margin-top: 10px; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Western Europe:</strong> Highest average cost of living (83.4) with low inflation (2.5%), driven by Swiss and UK cities. However, high wages provide partial buffer.</li>
                    <li><strong>North America:</strong> High cost of living (82.4) with elevated inflation (3.1%). US cities dominate high-wage categories but show significant rent pressure.</li>
                    <li><strong>South Asia:</strong> Lowest cost of living (29.6) but extreme rent burden (54.8% of salary). Cities like Dhaka and Karachi show rent-to-salary ratios near 50% with very low absolute salaries.</li>
                    <li><strong>Middle East:</strong> Mixed picture — Tel Aviv and Dubai are expensive hub cities, while Doha and Riyadh benefit from oil wealth and low living costs despite lower salaries.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['regional_comparison']}" alt="Regional Comparison">
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Affordability and Rent Burden -->
        <div class="section" id="affordability-analysis">
            <h2>Affordability and Rent Burden Analysis</h2>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Why Rent Burden is the Most Critical Metric:</strong> Housing cost as a share of income is the single most actionable metric for policymakers. It directly measures whether workers can afford to live near employment centers, and extremely high ratios (over 50%) indicate systemic unaffordability that forces long commutes, overcrowding, or household stress.
            </div>

            <h3>Top 15 Cities by Rent Burden</h3>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>City</th>
                    <th>Country</th>
                    <th>Rent-to-Salary Ratio</th>
                    <th>Monthly Rent (1BR)</th>
                    <th>Avg Monthly Salary</th>
                    <th>Crisis Tier</th>
                    <th>Crisis Label</th>
                </tr>
                {''.join(f"<tr class='tier-{row['cost_crisis_tier']}'><td>{row['city']}</td><td>{row['country']}</td><td><strong>{row['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct']:.1f}%</strong></td><td>${row['monthly_rent_1br_city_center_usd']:,}</td><td>${row['avg_monthly_net_salary_usd']:,}</td><td>Tier {row['cost_crisis_tier']}</td><td>{row['crisis_label']}</td></tr>" for _, row in high_rent.iterrows())}
            </table>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['top_rent_burden']}" alt="Top 15 Cities by Rent Burden">
            </div>

            <h3>Rent Burden by Crisis Tier</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['tier_rent']}" alt="Rent Burden by Crisis Tier">
            </div>

            <h3>Relationship Between Rent Index and Cost of Living</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['rent_coli']}" alt="Rent Index vs Cost of Living">
            </div>

            <div class="warning-box">
                <strong>Key Finding — Correlation: {corr_coli_rent:.3f}</strong><br>
                Cost of living and rent indices show a strong positive correlation ({corr_coli_rent:.3f}). However, the relationship is not uniform. Some high-cost cities (e.g., Zurich) maintain lower rent burdens relative to salary due to high wages, while mid-tier cities like Vancouver and Miami show severe rent pressure despite more moderate overall costs.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Crisis Tier Profiling -->
        <div class="section" id="crisis-tier-profiling">
            <h2>Crisis Tier Profiling</h2>

            <p>The dataset classifies cities into 5 crisis tiers based on a composite of economic stress indicators. The following profiles show the average characteristics of each tier.</p>

            <div class="tier-summary-grid">
                {''.join(f"""
                <div class="tier-card t{tier}">
                    <h4>Tier {tier}: {label}</h4>
                    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Cities:</span><span class="value">{count}</span></div>
                    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Avg CoL Index:</span><span class="value">{coli:.1f}</span></div>
                    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Avg Rent Burden:</span><span class="value">{rent:.1f}%</span></div>
                    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Avg PPP Index:</span><span class="value">{ppp:.1f}</span></div>
                    <div class="stat"><span class="label">Avg Inflation:</span><span class="value">{infl:.1f}%</span></div>
                </div>
                """ for tier, label, count, coli, rent, ppp, infl in [(row['cost_crisis_tier'], row['crisis_label'], int(row['city_count']), row['cost_of_living_index'], row['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct'], row['local_purchasing_power_index'], row['annual_inflation_rate_2025_pct']) for _, row in tier_summary.iterrows()])}
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['tier_distribution']}" alt="Crisis Tier Distribution">
            </div>

            <h3>What Distinguishes High-Crisis from Affluent Cities</h3>
            <div class="two-column">
                <div class="region-card">
                    <h4>Affluent Cities (Tier 1)</h4>
                    <p style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #666; margin-bottom: 15px;">Affluent – High Cost (5 cities)</p>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>High cost of living</span><span class="value">110.0+</span></div>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>High wages</span><span class="value">$6,500+</span></div>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>Lower rent burden</span><span class="value">~69%</span></div>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>High purchasing power</span><span class="value">163+</span></div>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>Low inflation</span><span class="value">~1.5%</span></div>
                    <p style="margin-top: 15px; font-size: 0.9em;"><strong>Example:</strong> Zurich, Geneva, Basel — Swiss cities with very high wages that offset expensive living costs.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="region-card" style="border-color: var(--danger);">
                    <h4 style="color: var(--danger);">High-Crisis Cities (Tier 4-5)</h4>
                    <p style="font-size: 0.9em; color: #666; margin-bottom: 15px;">Severe inflation/hyperinflation + Low incomes</p>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>Low cost of living</span><span class="value">26-38</span></div>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>Very low wages</span><span class="value">$165-$700</span></div>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>High rent burden</span><span class="value">52-57%</span></div>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>Low purchasing power</span><span class="value">42-58</span></div>
                    <div class="region-stat"><span>Extreme inflation</span><span class="value">14-150%</span></div>
                    <p style="margin-top: 15px; font-size: 0.9em;"><strong>Example:</strong> Lagos, Beirut, Buenos Aires, Cairo — cities where inflation erosion and low wages create near-impossible living conditions.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Inflation and Oil Shock -->
        <div class="section" id="inflation-oil-exposure">
            <h2>Inflation and Oil Shock Exposure</h2>

            <h3>Correlation Analysis</h3>
            <table>
                <tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Correlation Coefficient</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Inflation vs Purchasing Power</td><td>{corr_inflation_ppp:.3f}</td><td>Strong negative — higher inflation directly erodes purchasing power</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Cost of Living vs Rent Index</td><td>{corr_coli_rent:.3f}</td><td>Strong positive — cities with high costs also have high rents</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Cost of Living vs PPP</td><td>{corr_coli_ppp:.3f}</td><td>Moderate positive — wealthy cities can be expensive AND have high PPP</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Rent Burden vs Salary</td><td>{corr_rent_salary:.3f}</td><td>Weak negative — higher salaries slightly reduce rent burden ratios</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h3>Inflation vs Purchasing Power</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['inflation_ppp']}" alt="Inflation vs Purchasing Power">
            </div>

            <h3>High-Inflation Cities (Top 15)</h3>
            <table>
                <tr><th>City</th><th>Country</th><th>Inflation Rate</th><th>Crisis Label</th><th>Oil Exposure</th></tr>
                {''.join(f"<tr><td>{row['city']}</td><td>{row['country']}</td><td><strong>{row['annual_inflation_rate_2025_pct']:.1f}%</strong></td><td>{row['crisis_label']}</td><td><span class='badge badge-warning'>{row['oil_shock_exposure']}</span></td></tr>" for _, row in inflation_high.head(15).iterrows())}
            </table>

            <h3>Oil Shock Exposure Analysis</h3>
            <table>
                <tr><th>Oil Shock Exposure</th><th>Cities</th><th>Avg CoL Index</th><th>Avg Inflation</th><th>Avg PPP Index</th><th>Avg Rent Burden</th></tr>
                {''.join(f"<tr><td><strong>{exp}</strong></td><td>{data['city_count']}</td><td>{data['cost_of_living_index']:.1f}</td><td>{data['annual_inflation_rate_2025_pct']:.1f}%</td><td>{data['local_purchasing_power_index']:.1f}</td><td>{data['rent_to_salary_ratio_pct']:.1f}%</td></tr>" for exp, data in oil_summary.iterrows())}
            </table>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['oil_shock']}" alt="Oil Shock Exposure Analysis">
            </div>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Oil Shock Vulnerability:</strong> Cities with <strong>High</strong> oil shock exposure show higher average inflation (15.7%) compared to Low exposure (2.8%). However, cities like Doha and Riyadh turn this vulnerability into advantage — their oil wealth allows them to maintain low costs while offering higher-than-expected salaries relative to local prices.
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Key Findings -->
        <div class="section" id="key-findings-recommendations">
            <h2>Key Findings and Policy Implications</h2>

            <h3>Urgent Attention Required</h3>

            <div class="finding">
                <h4>1. Beirut, Lebanon — Post-Conflict Hyperinflation</h4>
                <p>With an inflation rate of <strong>50%</strong> and purchasing power index of only <strong>35</strong>, Beirut represents the intersection of conflict, currency collapse, and hyperinflation. Median salary ($600/month) cannot keep pace with any cost category.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="finding">
                <h4>2. South Asian Cities — Structural Affordability Crisis</h4>
                <p>Delhi, Mumbai, Dhaka, Karachi, and Kathmandu all appear in Tier 4-5 with rent burdens near 50-55% and salaries under $900/month. These cities face structural, not cyclical, affordability challenges. Policy interventions must focus on wage growth and social housing, not inflation control alone.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="finding">
                <h4>3. Argentine Cluster — Persistent Hyperinflation</h4>
                <p>Buenos Aires shows an extraordinary <strong>150% annual inflation</strong> — 3x higher than the next closest city (Lagos at 34%). This is a policy failure requiring international intervention mechanisms, not standard economic adjustment.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="finding">
                <h4>4. African Tier 5 Cities — Compound Vulnerability</h4>
                <p>Lagos (34% inflation), Accra (18% inflation), and Nairobi (6.8% inflation) face compound pressures: low incomes ($380-$600/month), high rent burdens (53-55%), and oil shock exposure. These cities lack the fiscal buffers to absorb external shocks.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Regional Priority Rankings for Intervention</h3>
            <table>
                <tr><th>Priority</th><th>Region</th><th>Rationale</th><th>Recommended Focus</th></tr>
                <tr><td><span class="badge badge-danger">1</span></td><td>West Africa</td><td>Lowest incomes, high inflation, high oil exposure</td><td>Emergency relief, wage subsidies</td></tr>
                <tr><td><span class="badge badge-danger">2</span></td><td>South Asia</td><td>Severe rent burden, low salaries, large populations</td><td>Housing policy, urban development</td></tr>
                <tr><td><span class="badge badge-warning">3</span></td><td>Eastern Europe</td><td>Moderate costs but rapidly rising inflation (Poland, Hungary)</td><td>Inflation protection, social safety nets</td></tr>
                <tr><td><span class="badge badge-warning">4</span></td><td>Middle East (Non-Oil)</td><td>Conflict zones (Beirut, Amman) with refugee pressures</td><td>Humanitarian funding, employment programs</td></tr>
                <tr><td><span class="badge badge-info">5</span></td><td>Latin America</td><td>Argentina hyperinflation, moderate elsewhere</td><td>Fiscal discipline, wage growth policies</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h3>Recommended Further Research</h3>
            <ul style="margin-left: 20px; line-height: 2;">
                <li><strong>Housing supply elasticity:</strong> Which cities have policy mechanisms to increase housing supply without triggering price bubbles?</li>
